		{
			"id": "8FySu5eB",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -513.2577211374141,
			"y": 13100.496279489298,
			"width": 960.8151245117188,
			"height": 20,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 1,
			"strokeStyle": "dashed",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bCb",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 747660010,
			"version": 329,
			"versionNonce": 561709558,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942920,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "This distributed lock pattern can be used for things like resource allocation, concurrency control, and clustered scheduling.",
			"rawText": "This distributed lock pattern can be used for things like resource allocation, concurrency control, and clustered scheduling.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "This distributed lock pattern can be used for things like resource allocation, concurrency control, and clustered scheduling.",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "oOvWOoDk",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -511.16934044348363,
			"y": 13150.522249428459,
			"width": 1669.7109375,
			"height": 20,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#e03131",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 1,
			"strokeStyle": "dashed",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bCc",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 857241002,
			"version": 344,
			"versionNonce": 301159222,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942920,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "While ZooKeeper's locks work well, they're not designed for high-frequency locking (hundreds of times per second). For such use cases, consider specialized solutions like Redis-based locks or database transactions.",
			"rawText": "While ZooKeeper's locks work well, they're not designed for high-frequency locking (hundreds of times per second). For such use cases, consider specialized solutions like Redis-based locks or database transactions.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "While ZooKeeper's locks work well, they're not designed for high-frequency locking (hundreds of times per second). For such use cases, consider specialized solutions like Redis-based locks or database transactions.",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "eAFNqmsv",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -508.266755630907,
			"y": 13221.410878147997,
			"width": 1685.1517644556657,
			"height": 80,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1971c2",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 1,
			"strokeStyle": "dashed",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bCd",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1016082538,
			"version": 700,
			"versionNonce": 164592758,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942920,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "When to choose ZooKeeper locks over Redis locks?\nIn our Ticketmaster and Uber breakdowns, we used Redis distributed locks for their superior performance and simplicity. Choose ZooKeeper locks instead when you need stronger consistency guarantees for critical\noperations where correctness trumps performance (like financial transactions). ZooKeeper is also preferable for long-lived locks (hours) where its automatic failure detection via ephemeral nodes provides more robust\nhandling of server crashes than Redis locks which would require careful timeout management and heartbeat mechanisms.",
			"rawText": "When to choose ZooKeeper locks over Redis locks?\nIn our Ticketmaster and Uber breakdowns, we used Redis distributed locks for their superior performance and simplicity. Choose ZooKeeper locks instead when you need stronger consistency guarantees for critical operations where correctness trumps performance (like financial transactions). ZooKeeper is also preferable for long-lived locks (hours) where its automatic failure detection via ephemeral nodes provides more robust handling of server crashes than Redis locks which would require careful timeout management and heartbeat mechanisms.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "When to choose ZooKeeper locks over Redis locks?\nIn our Ticketmaster and Uber breakdowns, we used Redis distributed locks for their superior performance and simplicity. Choose ZooKeeper locks instead when you need stronger consistency guarantees for critical operations where correctness trumps performance (like financial transactions). ZooKeeper is also preferable for long-lived locks (hours) where its automatic failure detection via ephemeral nodes provides more robust handling of server crashes than Redis locks which would require careful timeout management and heartbeat mechanisms.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "6tv7TIxC",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -509.0952162064441,
			"y": 13476.789703268374,
			"width": 298.8998718261719,
			"height": 35,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 1,
			"strokeStyle": "dashed",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bCe",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 482513706,
			"version": 406,
			"versionNonce": 517242294,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942920,
			"link": null,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "How ZooKeeper Works",
			"rawText": "How ZooKeeper Works",
			"fontSize": 28,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "How ZooKeeper Works",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false
		},
		{
			"id": "zJooK9Iv",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -507.20533505049343,
			"y": 13555.841964387228,
			"width": 202.4198455810547,
			"height": 25,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 1,
			"strokeStyle": "dashed",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bCf",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1981455850,
			"version": 435,
			"versionNonce": 1713854198,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942920,
			"link": null,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Consensus with ZAB",
			"rawText": "Consensus with ZAB",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Consensus with ZAB",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false
		},
		{
			"id": "fIUh3k00",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -502.2501044164951,
			"y": 13604.628848098155,
			"width": 1698.5618574264083,
			"height": 220,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 1,
			"strokeStyle": "dashed",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bCg",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1705201834,
			"version": 567,
			"versionNonce": 139266102,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942920,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "As we talked about earlier, ZooKeeper itself is made up on an ensemble of servers. In other words, there are multiple servers all running the same ZooKeeper software and coordinating with each other. There's a\nbeautiful irony here: ZooKeeper helps other applications solve their coordination problems, but first it had to solve its own coordination challenges as a distributed system itself.\nAt the heart of ZooKeeper is the ZooKeeper Atomic Broadcast (ZAB) protocol. ZAB is what enables all ZooKeeper servers to agree on the state of the system, even when servers fail or network issues occur.\n\nZAB works in two main phases:\n    1. Leader Election: When the ZooKeeper ensemble starts or the current leader fails, servers use a voting process to elect a new leader. The primary factor in leader election is having the most up-to-date\ntransaction history. If multiple servers have equivalent transaction histories, then the server with the highest ID will be preferred. (Confusingly, this is the opposite of the application-level leader election pattern we\nsaw earlier, where the node with the lowest sequential ZNode number becomes the leader—a perfect example of how ZooKeeper's internal mechanisms can differ from the patterns built on top of it.)\n\n    2. Atomic Broadcast: Once a leader is elected, all write requests go to the leader. The leader then broadcasts these changes to all followers. A write is only considered successful when a majority (quorum) of\nservers have persisted the change.",
			"rawText": "As we talked about earlier, ZooKeeper itself is made up on an ensemble of servers. In other words, there are multiple servers all running the same ZooKeeper software and coordinating with each other. There's a beautiful irony here: ZooKeeper helps other applications solve their coordination problems, but first it had to solve its own coordination challenges as a distributed system itself.\nAt the heart of ZooKeeper is the ZooKeeper Atomic Broadcast (ZAB) protocol. ZAB is what enables all ZooKeeper servers to agree on the state of the system, even when servers fail or network issues occur.\n\nZAB works in two main phases:\n    1. Leader Election: When the ZooKeeper ensemble starts or the current leader fails, servers use a voting process to elect a new leader. The primary factor in leader election is having the most up-to-date transaction history. If multiple servers have equivalent transaction histories, then the server with the highest ID will be preferred. (Confusingly, this is the opposite of the application-level leader election pattern we saw earlier, where the node with the lowest sequential ZNode number becomes the leader—a perfect example of how ZooKeeper's internal mechanisms can differ from the patterns built on top of it.)\n\n    2. Atomic Broadcast: Once a leader is elected, all write requests go to the leader. The leader then broadcasts these changes to all followers. A write is only considered successful when a majority (quorum) of servers have persisted the change.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "As we talked about earlier, ZooKeeper itself is made up on an ensemble of servers. In other words, there are multiple servers all running the same ZooKeeper software and coordinating with each other. There's a beautiful irony here: ZooKeeper helps other applications solve their coordination problems, but first it had to solve its own coordination challenges as a distributed system itself.\nAt the heart of ZooKeeper is the ZooKeeper Atomic Broadcast (ZAB) protocol. ZAB is what enables all ZooKeeper servers to agree on the state of the system, even when servers fail or network issues occur.\n\nZAB works in two main phases:\n    1. Leader Election: When the ZooKeeper ensemble starts or the current leader fails, servers use a voting process to elect a new leader. The primary factor in leader election is having the most up-to-date transaction history. If multiple servers have equivalent transaction histories, then the server with the highest ID will be preferred. (Confusingly, this is the opposite of the application-level leader election pattern we saw earlier, where the node with the lowest sequential ZNode number becomes the leader—a perfect example of how ZooKeeper's internal mechanisms can differ from the patterns built on top of it.)\n\n    2. Atomic Broadcast: Once a leader is elected, all write requests go to the leader. The leader then broadcasts these changes to all followers. A write is only considered successful when a majority (quorum) of servers have persisted the change.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
