			"strokeColor": "#1971c2",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bD2",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1588639018,
			"version": 328,
			"versionNonce": 410810998,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "ZAB is similar to the Paxos and Raft consensus algorithms you might have heard of. While ZAB predates Raft and has some implementation differences, the high-level goal is the same: achieving consensus in a distributed system.",
			"rawText": "ZAB is similar to the Paxos and Raft consensus algorithms you might have heard of. While ZAB predates Raft and has some implementation differences, the high-level goal is the same: achieving consensus in a distributed system.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "ZAB is similar to the Paxos and Raft consensus algorithms you might have heard of. While ZAB predates Raft and has some implementation differences, the high-level goal is the same: achieving consensus in a distributed system.",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "r1XliDAc",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -500.6153493640081,
			"y": 14975.270991711042,
			"width": 1745.1292600261356,
			"height": 40,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bD3",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1509195754,
			"version": 448,
			"versionNonce": 187808694,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "This two-phase protocol ensures that even if some servers crash or network partitions occur, the remaining servers will maintain a consistent view of the data. The quorum requirement (majority of servers) means that\nwith a 5-server ensemble, ZooKeeper can tolerate 2 server failures and still function correctly.",
			"rawText": "This two-phase protocol ensures that even if some servers crash or network partitions occur, the remaining servers will maintain a consistent view of the data. The quorum requirement (majority of servers) means that with a 5-server ensemble, ZooKeeper can tolerate 2 server failures and still function correctly.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "This two-phase protocol ensures that even if some servers crash or network partitions occur, the remaining servers will maintain a consistent view of the data. The quorum requirement (majority of servers) means that with a 5-server ensemble, ZooKeeper can tolerate 2 server failures and still function correctly.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "cDZc7JUO",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -506.0928049326799,
			"y": 15178.812510949892,
			"width": 307.0197448730469,
			"height": 25,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bD4",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 938537642,
			"version": 371,
			"versionNonce": 271373558,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"link": null,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Strong Consistency Guarantees",
			"rawText": "Strong Consistency Guarantees",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Strong Consistency Guarantees",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false
		},
		{
			"id": "I2OQhmbm",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -503.8559145730833,
			"y": 15229.78573711272,
			"width": 1826.2789237180275,
			"height": 240,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bD5",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1800473962,
			"version": 608,
			"versionNonce": 284345910,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "ZooKeeper provides several important consistency guarantees that make it reliable for distributed coordination:\n    1. Sequential Consistency: Updates from a client are applied in the order they were sent. If a client updates node A and then node B, all servers will see the update to A before the update to B.\n    2. Atomicity: Updates either succeed or fail completely. There are no partial updates.\n    3. Single System Image: Regardless of which server a client connects to, it will see the same view of the system (after synchronization). A client can read from any server, but all writes go through the leader.\n    4. Durability: Once an update is applied, it persists until overwritten by a client. Even if servers fail and restart, the updates are not lost.\n    5. Timeliness: The system guarantees that clients' views of the system are updated within a bounded amount of time.\n\nZooKeeper achieves these guarantees through its ZAB protocol. All writes funnel through the leader, establishing a total ordering of updates, and are only considered successful when a quorum of servers persists them to\ntransaction logs. The protocol uses a two-phase commit process where the leader proposes updates, waits for acknowledgments, and only then commits changes - ensuring atomicity and durability even if servers fail.\n\nFor maintaining the single system image, ZooKeeper uses version numbers for each ZNode and synchronization protocols that allow followers to catch up after disconnections. However, reads from followers can return stale data since\nthey don't consult the leader on every read. For the strongest consistency, clients can use the \"sync\" operation before reading to ensure they get the most up-to-date data.",
			"rawText": "ZooKeeper provides several important consistency guarantees that make it reliable for distributed coordination:\n    1. Sequential Consistency: Updates from a client are applied in the order they were sent. If a client updates node A and then node B, all servers will see the update to A before the update to B.\n    2. Atomicity: Updates either succeed or fail completely. There are no partial updates.\n    3. Single System Image: Regardless of which server a client connects to, it will see the same view of the system (after synchronization). A client can read from any server, but all writes go through the leader.\n    4. Durability: Once an update is applied, it persists until overwritten by a client. Even if servers fail and restart, the updates are not lost.\n    5. Timeliness: The system guarantees that clients' views of the system are updated within a bounded amount of time.\n\nZooKeeper achieves these guarantees through its ZAB protocol. All writes funnel through the leader, establishing a total ordering of updates, and are only considered successful when a quorum of servers persists them to transaction logs. The protocol uses a two-phase commit process where the leader proposes updates, waits for acknowledgments, and only then commits changes - ensuring atomicity and durability even if servers fail.\n\nFor maintaining the single system image, ZooKeeper uses version numbers for each ZNode and synchronization protocols that allow followers to catch up after disconnections. However, reads from followers can return stale data since they don't consult the leader on every read. For the strongest consistency, clients can use the \"sync\" operation before reading to ensure they get the most up-to-date data.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "ZooKeeper provides several important consistency guarantees that make it reliable for distributed coordination:\n    1. Sequential Consistency: Updates from a client are applied in the order they were sent. If a client updates node A and then node B, all servers will see the update to A before the update to B.\n    2. Atomicity: Updates either succeed or fail completely. There are no partial updates.\n    3. Single System Image: Regardless of which server a client connects to, it will see the same view of the system (after synchronization). A client can read from any server, but all writes go through the leader.\n    4. Durability: Once an update is applied, it persists until overwritten by a client. Even if servers fail and restart, the updates are not lost.\n    5. Timeliness: The system guarantees that clients' views of the system are updated within a bounded amount of time.\n\nZooKeeper achieves these guarantees through its ZAB protocol. All writes funnel through the leader, establishing a total ordering of updates, and are only considered successful when a quorum of servers persists them to transaction logs. The protocol uses a two-phase commit process where the leader proposes updates, waits for acknowledgments, and only then commits changes - ensuring atomicity and durability even if servers fail.\n\nFor maintaining the single system image, ZooKeeper uses version numbers for each ZNode and synchronization protocols that allow followers to catch up after disconnections. However, reads from followers can return stale data since they don't consult the leader on every read. For the strongest consistency, clients can use the \"sync\" operation before reading to ensure they get the most up-to-date data.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "pNgC0Dlt",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -502.9295756972019,
			"y": 15499.104791412288,
			"width": 1624.622802734375,
			"height": 20,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#e03131",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bD6",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1499451434,
			"version": 328,
			"versionNonce": 2120781686,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "While ZooKeeper provides strong consistency, it is not designed for high-throughput read/write scenarios. It's optimized for workloads with more reads than writes and where the data size is relatively small.",
			"rawText": "While ZooKeeper provides strong consistency, it is not designed for high-throughput read/write scenarios. It's optimized for workloads with more reads than writes and where the data size is relatively small.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "While ZooKeeper provides strong consistency, it is not designed for high-throughput read/write scenarios. It's optimized for workloads with more reads than writes and where the data size is relatively small.",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "erQPLqqI",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -497.70611063852675,
			"y": 15653.872408103189,
			"width": 262.559814453125,
			"height": 25,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bD7",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 101202666,
			"version": 331,
			"versionNonce": 1691562166,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Read and Write Operations",
			"rawText": "Read and Write Operations",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Read and Write Operations",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "PzM71Evu",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -500.350489824481,
			"y": 15728.273235118091,
			"width": 1791.7658693265578,
			"height": 180,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bD8",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1070943658,
			"version": 486,
			"versionNonce": 2144840182,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "ZooKeeper's architecture is specifically optimized for read-dominant workloads. This design choice influences how read and write operations are handled:\n    1. Read Operations: Any server in the ensemble can serve read requests directly from its in-memory copy of the data. This provides high throughput and low latency for reads, which is why ZooKeeper excels at \"read-dominant\"\nworkloads with ratios of around 10:1 reads to writes.\n    2. Write Operations: All write requests must go through the leader, which coordinates the update across the ensemble using the ZAB protocol. This centralized approach ensures consistent ordering but makes writes more\nexpensive than reads.\n\nFor our chat application, this means:\n    - When Server 1 needs to find where Bob is connected (a read operation), it can query any ZooKeeper server.\n    - When Server 3 needs to update Bob's location (a write operation), this request goes to the leader, which then synchronizes it across the ensemble.",
			"rawText": "ZooKeeper's architecture is specifically optimized for read-dominant workloads. This design choice influences how read and write operations are handled:\n    1. Read Operations: Any server in the ensemble can serve read requests directly from its in-memory copy of the data. This provides high throughput and low latency for reads, which is why ZooKeeper excels at \"read-dominant\" workloads with ratios of around 10:1 reads to writes.\n    2. Write Operations: All write requests must go through the leader, which coordinates the update across the ensemble using the ZAB protocol. This centralized approach ensures consistent ordering but makes writes more expensive than reads.\n\nFor our chat application, this means:\n    - When Server 1 needs to find where Bob is connected (a read operation), it can query any ZooKeeper server.\n    - When Server 3 needs to update Bob's location (a write operation), this request goes to the leader, which then synchronizes it across the ensemble.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "ZooKeeper's architecture is specifically optimized for read-dominant workloads. This design choice influences how read and write operations are handled:\n    1. Read Operations: Any server in the ensemble can serve read requests directly from its in-memory copy of the data. This provides high throughput and low latency for reads, which is why ZooKeeper excels at \"read-dominant\" workloads with ratios of around 10:1 reads to writes.\n    2. Write Operations: All write requests must go through the leader, which coordinates the update across the ensemble using the ZAB protocol. This centralized approach ensures consistent ordering but makes writes more expensive than reads.\n\nFor our chat application, this means:\n    - When Server 1 needs to find where Bob is connected (a read operation), it can query any ZooKeeper server.\n    - When Server 3 needs to update Bob's location (a write operation), this request goes to the leader, which then synchronizes it across the ensemble.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "iPxyC1lp",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -511.1901433854532,
			"y": 15962.8123518197,
			"width": 1823.4238008062523,
			"height": 40,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bD9",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1274153066,
			"version": 471,
			"versionNonce": 1316682550,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"link": null,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Because reads are served locally by each server, it's possible for a client to read stale data if it connects to a follower that hasn't yet synchronized with the leader. For applications requiring the strongest consistency, ZooKeeper\nprovides \"sync\" operations that ensure a server is up-to-date before performing a read.",
			"rawText": "Because reads are served locally by each server, it's possible for a client to read stale data if it connects to a follower that hasn't yet synchronized with the leader. For applications requiring the strongest consistency, ZooKeeper provides \"sync\" operations that ensure a server is up-to-date before performing a read.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Because reads are served locally by each server, it's possible for a client to read stale data if it connects to a follower that hasn't yet synchronized with the leader. For applications requiring the strongest consistency, ZooKeeper provides \"sync\" operations that ensure a server is up-to-date before performing a read.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false
		},
		{
			"id": "kwvx0nCK",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -490.6909281233243,
			"y": 16153.59485994023,
			"width": 362.49969482421875,
			"height": 25,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bDA",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 304704298,
			"version": 504,
			"versionNonce": 656548982,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"link": null,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Sessions and Connection Management",
			"rawText": "Sessions and Connection Management",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Sessions and Connection Management",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false
		},
		{
			"id": "RLggtBFu",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -482.2764845550496,
			"y": 16229.713481958808,
			"width": 1810.8134385821954,
			"height": 240,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bDB",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1223063018,
			"version": 485,
			"versionNonce": 1267869110,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "ZooKeeper uses the concept of sessions to manage client connections and maintain ephemeral nodes. Sessions are crucial for detecting client failures and implementing features like ephemeral nodes. \n    - 1. Session Establishment: When a client connects to ZooKeeper, it establishes a session with a configurable timeout (typically 10-30 seconds).\n    - 2. Heartbeats: The client sends periodic heartbeats to maintain its session. If ZooKeeper doesn't receive a heartbeat within the timeout period, it assumes the client has failed.\n    - 3. Session Recovery: If a client loses its connection to a ZooKeeper server, it can connect to a different server and recover its session, as long as it does so before the session times out.\n    - 4. Session Expiration: If a session expires, all ephemeral nodes created by that client are automatically deleted, and all watches registered by that client are removed.\n\nFor our chat application, this session mechanism provides automatic cleanup when servers or users disconnect unexpectedly:\n    - 1. Server 2 establishes a session with ZooKeeper when it starts\n    - 2. It creates ephemeral nodes for itself and connected users\n    - 3. If Server 2 crashes, its session eventually times out\n    - 4. ZooKeeper automatically removes all ephemeral nodes owned by Server 2\n    - 5. Other servers watching these nodes are notified about users going offline",
			"rawText": "ZooKeeper uses the concept of sessions to manage client connections and maintain ephemeral nodes. Sessions are crucial for detecting client failures and implementing features like ephemeral nodes. \n    - 1. Session Establishment: When a client connects to ZooKeeper, it establishes a session with a configurable timeout (typically 10-30 seconds).\n    - 2. Heartbeats: The client sends periodic heartbeats to maintain its session. If ZooKeeper doesn't receive a heartbeat within the timeout period, it assumes the client has failed.\n    - 3. Session Recovery: If a client loses its connection to a ZooKeeper server, it can connect to a different server and recover its session, as long as it does so before the session times out.\n    - 4. Session Expiration: If a session expires, all ephemeral nodes created by that client are automatically deleted, and all watches registered by that client are removed.\n\nFor our chat application, this session mechanism provides automatic cleanup when servers or users disconnect unexpectedly:\n    - 1. Server 2 establishes a session with ZooKeeper when it starts\n    - 2. It creates ephemeral nodes for itself and connected users\n    - 3. If Server 2 crashes, its session eventually times out\n    - 4. ZooKeeper automatically removes all ephemeral nodes owned by Server 2\n    - 5. Other servers watching these nodes are notified about users going offline",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "ZooKeeper uses the concept of sessions to manage client connections and maintain ephemeral nodes. Sessions are crucial for detecting client failures and implementing features like ephemeral nodes. \n    - 1. Session Establishment: When a client connects to ZooKeeper, it establishes a session with a configurable timeout (typically 10-30 seconds).\n    - 2. Heartbeats: The client sends periodic heartbeats to maintain its session. If ZooKeeper doesn't receive a heartbeat within the timeout period, it assumes the client has failed.\n    - 3. Session Recovery: If a client loses its connection to a ZooKeeper server, it can connect to a different server and recover its session, as long as it does so before the session times out.\n    - 4. Session Expiration: If a session expires, all ephemeral nodes created by that client are automatically deleted, and all watches registered by that client are removed.\n\nFor our chat application, this session mechanism provides automatic cleanup when servers or users disconnect unexpectedly:\n    - 1. Server 2 establishes a session with ZooKeeper when it starts\n    - 2. It creates ephemeral nodes for itself and connected users\n    - 3. If Server 2 crashes, its session eventually times out\n    - 4. ZooKeeper automatically removes all ephemeral nodes owned by Server 2\n    - 5. Other servers watching these nodes are notified about users going offline",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "igOL6IXp",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -483.34943284985457,
			"y": 16505.339896089547,
			"width": 1584.7497077341857,
			"height": 20,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#e03131",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bDC",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 557066410,
			"version": 564,
			"versionNonce": 2096103158,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"link": null,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Configuring the right session timeout is critical. Too short, and temporary network issues might cause unnecessary session expirations. Too long, and your system will be slow to detect actual failures.",
			"rawText": "Configuring the right session timeout is critical. Too short, and temporary network issues might cause unnecessary session expirations. Too long, and your system will be slow to detect actual failures.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Configuring the right session timeout is critical. Too short, and temporary network issues might cause unnecessary session expirations. Too long, and your system will be slow to detect actual failures.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false
		},
		{
			"id": "OPUBTkuZ",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -479.3107015359192,
			"y": 16630.630994573803,
			"width": 204.83984375,
			"height": 25,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
