		{
			"id": "jOlvEm38",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -483.9577873708108,
			"y": 16685.068028240796,
			"width": 1817.223699304303,
			"height": 160,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bDE",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1741522474,
			"version": 515,
			"versionNonce": 2145991030,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "What about durability? How does ZooKeeper ensure that data is not lost, especially if a server in the ensemble crashes?\n    - 1. Transaction Log: Every state change (transaction) is first written to a transaction log on persistent storage. This write-ahead logging ensures that no acknowledged update is ever lost, even if a server crashes immediately\nafter confirming the update.\n    - 2. Snapshots: Periodically, ZooKeeper creates snapshots of its in-memory database to speed up recovery. When a server restarts, it loads the most recent snapshot and then replays transaction logs to recover the complete\nstate.\n\nThe ZooKeeper documentation emphasizes that the transaction log is the most performance-critical part of ZooKeeper:\n\"The most performance-critical part of ZooKeeper is the transaction log. ZooKeeper must sync transactions to media before it returns a response. A dedicated transaction log device is key to consistent good performance.\"",
			"rawText": "What about durability? How does ZooKeeper ensure that data is not lost, especially if a server in the ensemble crashes?\n    - 1. Transaction Log: Every state change (transaction) is first written to a transaction log on persistent storage. This write-ahead logging ensures that no acknowledged update is ever lost, even if a server crashes immediately after confirming the update.\n    - 2. Snapshots: Periodically, ZooKeeper creates snapshots of its in-memory database to speed up recovery. When a server restarts, it loads the most recent snapshot and then replays transaction logs to recover the complete state.\n\nThe ZooKeeper documentation emphasizes that the transaction log is the most performance-critical part of ZooKeeper:\n\"The most performance-critical part of ZooKeeper is the transaction log. ZooKeeper must sync transactions to media before it returns a response. A dedicated transaction log device is key to consistent good performance.\"",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "What about durability? How does ZooKeeper ensure that data is not lost, especially if a server in the ensemble crashes?\n    - 1. Transaction Log: Every state change (transaction) is first written to a transaction log on persistent storage. This write-ahead logging ensures that no acknowledged update is ever lost, even if a server crashes immediately after confirming the update.\n    - 2. Snapshots: Periodically, ZooKeeper creates snapshots of its in-memory database to speed up recovery. When a server restarts, it loads the most recent snapshot and then replays transaction logs to recover the complete state.\n\nThe ZooKeeper documentation emphasizes that the transaction log is the most performance-critical part of ZooKeeper:\n\"The most performance-critical part of ZooKeeper is the transaction log. ZooKeeper must sync transactions to media before it returns a response. A dedicated transaction log device is key to consistent good performance.\"",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "Qb3UlcXm",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -483.1905909403181,
			"y": 16886.495751375143,
			"width": 1751.326904296875,
			"height": 20,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#e03131",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bDF",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 693055722,
			"version": 408,
			"versionNonce": 78551734,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Memory swapping can severely impact ZooKeeper performance because all operations are ordered. If one request hits disk due to swapping, all queued requests will be delayed. Proper heap sizing is essential to avoid swapping.",
			"rawText": "Memory swapping can severely impact ZooKeeper performance because all operations are ordered. If one request hits disk due to swapping, all queued requests will be delayed. Proper heap sizing is essential to avoid swapping.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Memory swapping can severely impact ZooKeeper performance because all operations are ordered. If one request hits disk due to swapping, all queued requests will be delayed. Proper heap sizing is essential to avoid swapping.",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "8RPmTWye",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -483.2019495078134,
			"y": 17045.608130447028,
			"width": 158.11985778808594,
			"height": 25,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bDG",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 181240746,
			"version": 452,
			"versionNonce": 615896054,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"link": null,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Handling Failures",
			"rawText": "Handling Failures",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Handling Failures",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false
		},
		{
			"id": "cipNBxD9",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -484.2556881124383,
			"y": 17109.198597988936,
			"width": 2258.7822265625,
			"height": 360,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bDH",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 913538666,
			"version": 424,
			"versionNonce": 1241769270,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "What happens if a ZooKeeper server within the ensemble fails?\n    - 1. Server Failures: If a follower fails, the leader continues to operate as long as a quorum of servers remains available. If the leader fails, a new leader election occurs automatically by promoting the follower with the highest ID.\n    - 2. Network Partitions: If the network is partitioned such that no group has a majority of servers, ZooKeeper will not process write requests until the partition is resolved. This prevents the \"split-brain\" problem where different parts of the system disagree about the current state.\n    - 3. Client Failures: If a client fails, any ephemeral nodes it created are automatically removed after its session times out. This would be important for our chat application - if Server 2 crashes, all users connected to it are automatically marked as offline when their ZNodes disappear.\n    - 4. Client Session Management: ZooKeeper tracks client sessions and provides mechanisms for clients to recover and reconnect after temporary disconnections, without losing their session state or watches.\n\nHere's what happens in our chat application when a server fails:\n    - 1. Server 2 crashes\n    - 2. Server 2's ZooKeeper session times out (typically after 10-30 seconds)\n    - 3. All ephemeral nodes created by Server 2 are automatically deleted, including:\n           - /chat-app/servers/server2\n           - All /chat-app/users/X where X was connected to Server 2\n    - 4. Other servers with watches on these nodes receive notifications\n    - 5. They update their routing tables to mark users as offline\n    - 6. When a new Server 3 comes back online, it creates a new session and registers itself\n\nThis automatic failure handling is one of ZooKeeper's most powerful features. It allows distributed systems to recover from failures without manual intervention, maintaining consistency throughout.\n",
			"rawText": "What happens if a ZooKeeper server within the ensemble fails?\n    - 1. Server Failures: If a follower fails, the leader continues to operate as long as a quorum of servers remains available. If the leader fails, a new leader election occurs automatically by promoting the follower with the highest ID.\n    - 2. Network Partitions: If the network is partitioned such that no group has a majority of servers, ZooKeeper will not process write requests until the partition is resolved. This prevents the \"split-brain\" problem where different parts of the system disagree about the current state.\n    - 3. Client Failures: If a client fails, any ephemeral nodes it created are automatically removed after its session times out. This would be important for our chat application - if Server 2 crashes, all users connected to it are automatically marked as offline when their ZNodes disappear.\n    - 4. Client Session Management: ZooKeeper tracks client sessions and provides mechanisms for clients to recover and reconnect after temporary disconnections, without losing their session state or watches.\n\nHere's what happens in our chat application when a server fails:\n    - 1. Server 2 crashes\n    - 2. Server 2's ZooKeeper session times out (typically after 10-30 seconds)\n    - 3. All ephemeral nodes created by Server 2 are automatically deleted, including:\n           - /chat-app/servers/server2\n           - All /chat-app/users/X where X was connected to Server 2\n    - 4. Other servers with watches on these nodes receive notifications\n    - 5. They update their routing tables to mark users as offline\n    - 6. When a new Server 3 comes back online, it creates a new session and registers itself\n\nThis automatic failure handling is one of ZooKeeper's most powerful features. It allows distributed systems to recover from failures without manual intervention, maintaining consistency throughout.\n",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "What happens if a ZooKeeper server within the ensemble fails?\n    - 1. Server Failures: If a follower fails, the leader continues to operate as long as a quorum of servers remains available. If the leader fails, a new leader election occurs automatically by promoting the follower with the highest ID.\n    - 2. Network Partitions: If the network is partitioned such that no group has a majority of servers, ZooKeeper will not process write requests until the partition is resolved. This prevents the \"split-brain\" problem where different parts of the system disagree about the current state.\n    - 3. Client Failures: If a client fails, any ephemeral nodes it created are automatically removed after its session times out. This would be important for our chat application - if Server 2 crashes, all users connected to it are automatically marked as offline when their ZNodes disappear.\n    - 4. Client Session Management: ZooKeeper tracks client sessions and provides mechanisms for clients to recover and reconnect after temporary disconnections, without losing their session state or watches.\n\nHere's what happens in our chat application when a server fails:\n    - 1. Server 2 crashes\n    - 2. Server 2's ZooKeeper session times out (typically after 10-30 seconds)\n    - 3. All ephemeral nodes created by Server 2 are automatically deleted, including:\n           - /chat-app/servers/server2\n           - All /chat-app/users/X where X was connected to Server 2\n    - 4. Other servers with watches on these nodes receive notifications\n    - 5. They update their routing tables to mark users as offline\n    - 6. When a new Server 3 comes back online, it creates a new session and registers itself\n\nThis automatic failure handling is one of ZooKeeper's most powerful features. It allows distributed systems to recover from failures without manual intervention, maintaining consistency throughout.\n",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "9dLNzbwg",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -477.6037287462136,
			"y": 17483.654603362636,
			"width": 1883.9646922123814,
			"height": 20,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bDI",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1493967146,
			"version": 717,
			"versionNonce": 1442696822,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"link": null,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "ZooKeeper's session timeout is a crucial configuration parameter. Set it too low, and temporary network issues might cause unnecessary failovers. Set it too high, and your system will take longer to detect and respond to actual failures.",
			"rawText": "ZooKeeper's session timeout is a crucial configuration parameter. Set it too low, and temporary network issues might cause unnecessary failovers. Set it too high, and your system will take longer to detect and respond to actual failures.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "ZooKeeper's session timeout is a crucial configuration parameter. Set it too low, and temporary network issues might cause unnecessary failovers. Set it too high, and your system will take longer to detect and respond to actual failures.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false
		},
		{
