			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"link": null,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Limitations",
			"rawText": "Limitations",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Limitations",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false
		},
		{
			"id": "pM28hNxz",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -480.4890801052825,
			"y": 18618.89464582226,
			"width": 1884.4157700961268,
			"height": 180,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bDQ",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 693688106,
			"version": 400,
			"versionNonce": 252672118,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Hot Spotting Issues: When many clients watch the same ZNode (common in leader election or locks), servers can be overwhelmed with notification traffic. At scale, popular nodes become bottlenecks—imagine millions of users coming online\nsimultaneously in our chat example.\n\nPerformance Limitations: ZooKeeper's consistency model makes writes expensive as they must propagate through the leader to a quorum. Its in-memory storage model limits data capacity—keep ZNodes under 1MB and ensure your dataset\nfits in memory.\n\nOperational Complexity: ZooKeeper requires careful configuration of Java parameters, disk layouts, and ongoing monitoring of timeouts and connections. As one maintainer put it: \"ZooKeeper is simple to use but complex to operate.\"\n\nIf your design requires storing large amounts of data, handling extremely high write loads, or minimizing operational complexity, you might want to consider alternatives.",
			"rawText": "Hot Spotting Issues: When many clients watch the same ZNode (common in leader election or locks), servers can be overwhelmed with notification traffic. At scale, popular nodes become bottlenecks—imagine millions of users coming online simultaneously in our chat example.\n\nPerformance Limitations: ZooKeeper's consistency model makes writes expensive as they must propagate through the leader to a quorum. Its in-memory storage model limits data capacity—keep ZNodes under 1MB and ensure your dataset fits in memory.\n\nOperational Complexity: ZooKeeper requires careful configuration of Java parameters, disk layouts, and ongoing monitoring of timeouts and connections. As one maintainer put it: \"ZooKeeper is simple to use but complex to operate.\"\n\nIf your design requires storing large amounts of data, handling extremely high write loads, or minimizing operational complexity, you might want to consider alternatives.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Hot Spotting Issues: When many clients watch the same ZNode (common in leader election or locks), servers can be overwhelmed with notification traffic. At scale, popular nodes become bottlenecks—imagine millions of users coming online simultaneously in our chat example.\n\nPerformance Limitations: ZooKeeper's consistency model makes writes expensive as they must propagate through the leader to a quorum. Its in-memory storage model limits data capacity—keep ZNodes under 1MB and ensure your dataset fits in memory.\n\nOperational Complexity: ZooKeeper requires careful configuration of Java parameters, disk layouts, and ongoing monitoring of timeouts and connections. As one maintainer put it: \"ZooKeeper is simple to use but complex to operate.\"\n\nIf your design requires storing large amounts of data, handling extremely high write loads, or minimizing operational complexity, you might want to consider alternatives.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "bKy097L2",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -488.01657853896677,
			"y": 18979.120523958565,
			"width": 403.05975341796875,
			"height": 25,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bDR",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 524837354,
			"version": 472,
			"versionNonce": 1924463030,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"link": null,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "So when should you use ZooKeeper then?",
			"rawText": "So when should you use ZooKeeper then?",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "So when should you use ZooKeeper then?",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false
		},
		{
			"id": "TDUB7MPZ",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -487.5132319502427,
			"y": 19049.2404867997,
			"width": 2121.38450962806,
			"height": 240,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bDS",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 746099882,
			"version": 598,
			"versionNonce": 1620595446,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Smart Routing\n\nIn our chat application example, we've discussed how ZooKeeper helps route messages to the right server. But there's an even more sophisticated use case here. Even if we replaced ZooKeeper with a pub/sub broadcasting system for message delivery, we face an\nimportant optimization problem: limiting the number of channels or topics each server subscribes to. For optimal performance, we want users from the same chat room to be collocated on the same server. In other words, all websocket connections for a given chat room\nshould be handled by the same server (to the extent possible).\n\nThis colocation strategy becomes even more important when designing systems like Facebook Live Comments or YouTube Live Chat. When millions of users are watching the same live video, having all viewers of that stream connected to the same server (or server group)\nminimizes cross-server communication overhead. ZooKeeper serves as the coordination point for your API gateway, maintaining a mapping of which chat rooms or live streams are handled by which servers. When a new user connects, your gateway queries ZooKeeper to\ndetermine the optimal server for that user based on their chat room or live stream ID.\n\nThe practical implementation might look like this: each server registers in ZooKeeper with its capacity and the list of rooms it's handling. Your API gateway, when receiving a new connection, queries ZooKeeper to find the appropriate server based on the user's intended\nroom, then directs the user to connect there. If all servers handling a popular room reach capacity, ZooKeeper helps select a new server to expand the room's footprint in a coordinated way.",
			"rawText": "Smart Routing\n\nIn our chat application example, we've discussed how ZooKeeper helps route messages to the right server. But there's an even more sophisticated use case here. Even if we replaced ZooKeeper with a pub/sub broadcasting system for message delivery, we face an important optimization problem: limiting the number of channels or topics each server subscribes to. For optimal performance, we want users from the same chat room to be collocated on the same server. In other words, all websocket connections for a given chat room should be handled by the same server (to the extent possible).\n\nThis colocation strategy becomes even more important when designing systems like Facebook Live Comments or YouTube Live Chat. When millions of users are watching the same live video, having all viewers of that stream connected to the same server (or server group) minimizes cross-server communication overhead. ZooKeeper serves as the coordination point for your API gateway, maintaining a mapping of which chat rooms or live streams are handled by which servers. When a new user connects, your gateway queries ZooKeeper to determine the optimal server for that user based on their chat room or live stream ID.\n\nThe practical implementation might look like this: each server registers in ZooKeeper with its capacity and the list of rooms it's handling. Your API gateway, when receiving a new connection, queries ZooKeeper to find the appropriate server based on the user's intended room, then directs the user to connect there. If all servers handling a popular room reach capacity, ZooKeeper helps select a new server to expand the room's footprint in a coordinated way.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Smart Routing\n\nIn our chat application example, we've discussed how ZooKeeper helps route messages to the right server. But there's an even more sophisticated use case here. Even if we replaced ZooKeeper with a pub/sub broadcasting system for message delivery, we face an important optimization problem: limiting the number of channels or topics each server subscribes to. For optimal performance, we want users from the same chat room to be collocated on the same server. In other words, all websocket connections for a given chat room should be handled by the same server (to the extent possible).\n\nThis colocation strategy becomes even more important when designing systems like Facebook Live Comments or YouTube Live Chat. When millions of users are watching the same live video, having all viewers of that stream connected to the same server (or server group) minimizes cross-server communication overhead. ZooKeeper serves as the coordination point for your API gateway, maintaining a mapping of which chat rooms or live streams are handled by which servers. When a new user connects, your gateway queries ZooKeeper to determine the optimal server for that user based on their chat room or live stream ID.\n\nThe practical implementation might look like this: each server registers in ZooKeeper with its capacity and the list of rooms it's handling. Your API gateway, when receiving a new connection, queries ZooKeeper to find the appropriate server based on the user's intended room, then directs the user to connect there. If all servers handling a popular room reach capacity, ZooKeeper helps select a new server to expand the room's footprint in a coordinated way.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
