			"text": "SSE",
			"rawText": "SSE",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "center",
			"verticalAlign": "middle",
			"containerId": "DaLS2M4eniudxYlxypFfL",
			"originalText": "SSE",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "xUQ1QHt6",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -482.77925114857544,
			"y": 20027.91627325874,
			"width": 2067.4505668324755,
			"height": 160,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bDh",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 2119323114,
			"version": 590,
			"versionNonce": 2053284662,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Certain Infrastructure Design Problems\n\nZooKeeper becomes particularly relevant in deep infrastructure system design interviews, such as \"Design a distributed message queue\" or \"Design a distributed task scheduler.\" For these questions, ZooKeeper serves as the consensus \"brain\" of your system, just\nas Kafka historically used it. In a distributed message queue design, ZooKeeper would handle critical coordination functions:\n\nWhen a broker joins the cluster, it registers itself in ZooKeeper, allowing other components to discover it. Topic creation involves writing metadata to ZooKeeper, including the number of partitions and their replication factor. The critical leader election process\nfor each partition relies on ZooKeeper's sequential ephemeral nodes to ensure only one broker serves as the leader for each partition. Consumer groups track their membership and partition assignments in ZooKeeper, enabling rebalancing when consumers join or\nleave. And perhaps most importantly, broker failure detection happens through ZooKeeper's ephemeral nodes - when a broker dies, its session expires, and its nodes disappear, triggering reassignment of its partitions.",
			"rawText": "Certain Infrastructure Design Problems\n\nZooKeeper becomes particularly relevant in deep infrastructure system design interviews, such as \"Design a distributed message queue\" or \"Design a distributed task scheduler.\" For these questions, ZooKeeper serves as the consensus \"brain\" of your system, just as Kafka historically used it. In a distributed message queue design, ZooKeeper would handle critical coordination functions:\n\nWhen a broker joins the cluster, it registers itself in ZooKeeper, allowing other components to discover it. Topic creation involves writing metadata to ZooKeeper, including the number of partitions and their replication factor. The critical leader election process for each partition relies on ZooKeeper's sequential ephemeral nodes to ensure only one broker serves as the leader for each partition. Consumer groups track their membership and partition assignments in ZooKeeper, enabling rebalancing when consumers join or leave. And perhaps most importantly, broker failure detection happens through ZooKeeper's ephemeral nodes - when a broker dies, its session expires, and its nodes disappear, triggering reassignment of its partitions.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Certain Infrastructure Design Problems\n\nZooKeeper becomes particularly relevant in deep infrastructure system design interviews, such as \"Design a distributed message queue\" or \"Design a distributed task scheduler.\" For these questions, ZooKeeper serves as the consensus \"brain\" of your system, just as Kafka historically used it. In a distributed message queue design, ZooKeeper would handle critical coordination functions:\n\nWhen a broker joins the cluster, it registers itself in ZooKeeper, allowing other components to discover it. Topic creation involves writing metadata to ZooKeeper, including the number of partitions and their replication factor. The critical leader election process for each partition relies on ZooKeeper's sequential ephemeral nodes to ensure only one broker serves as the leader for each partition. Consumer groups track their membership and partition assignments in ZooKeeper, enabling rebalancing when consumers join or leave. And perhaps most importantly, broker failure detection happens through ZooKeeper's ephemeral nodes - when a broker dies, its session expires, and its nodes disappear, triggering reassignment of its partitions.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
