			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Distributed Message Queue",
			"rawText": "Distributed Message Queue",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Distributed Message Queue",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "zofL7EUc",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -482.5453766400251,
			"y": 21038.830381451397,
			"width": 2262.3984375,
			"height": 20,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bE7",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 491568490,
			"version": 347,
			"versionNonce": 1193085878,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "As mentioned, this exact pattern powered Kafka for years before KRaft, and similar approaches are used in other distributed systems like HBase, Hadoop, and Solr. When designing such systems, ZooKeeper provides a battle-tested solution for the complex coordination problems they face.",
			"rawText": "As mentioned, this exact pattern powered Kafka for years before KRaft, and similar approaches are used in other distributed systems like HBase, Hadoop, and Solr. When designing such systems, ZooKeeper provides a battle-tested solution for the complex coordination problems they face.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "As mentioned, this exact pattern powered Kafka for years before KRaft, and similar approaches are used in other distributed systems like HBase, Hadoop, and Solr. When designing such systems, ZooKeeper provides a battle-tested solution for the complex coordination problems they face.",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "1ztO2puA",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -486.6129587789537,
			"y": 21250.257666262263,
			"width": 1821.98814682938,
			"height": 136.48729659704622,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bE8",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 811958314,
			"version": 532,
			"versionNonce": 1378755830,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Durable Distributed Locks\n\nWhile Redis can handle distributed locks for many use cases like ticketing systems, ZooKeeper is strictly better for scenarios requiring hierarchical locks with complex dependencies. For example, in a distributed file system, ZooKeeper\nexcels when you need nested lock acquisition (like locking a directory and its files) with deadlock prevention. ZooKeeper's ability to maintain watch notifications on multiple nodes simultaneously allows clients to monitor the entire lock\nhierarchy, receiving immediate notifications about any changes in the lock structure. This is particularly valuable in systems where resources have parent-child relationships and lock acquisition must respect these hierarchies to prevent\ndeadlocks and ensure data integrity across distributed components.\n",
			"rawText": "Durable Distributed Locks\n\nWhile Redis can handle distributed locks for many use cases like ticketing systems, ZooKeeper is strictly better for scenarios requiring hierarchical locks with complex dependencies. For example, in a distributed file system, ZooKeeper excels when you need nested lock acquisition (like locking a directory and its files) with deadlock prevention. ZooKeeper's ability to maintain watch notifications on multiple nodes simultaneously allows clients to monitor the entire lock hierarchy, receiving immediate notifications about any changes in the lock structure. This is particularly valuable in systems where resources have parent-child relationships and lock acquisition must respect these hierarchies to prevent deadlocks and ensure data integrity across distributed components.\n",
			"fontSize": 15.598548182519568,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Durable Distributed Locks\n\nWhile Redis can handle distributed locks for many use cases like ticketing systems, ZooKeeper is strictly better for scenarios requiring hierarchical locks with complex dependencies. For example, in a distributed file system, ZooKeeper excels when you need nested lock acquisition (like locking a directory and its files) with deadlock prevention. ZooKeeper's ability to maintain watch notifications on multiple nodes simultaneously allows clients to monitor the entire lock hierarchy, receiving immediate notifications about any changes in the lock structure. This is particularly valuable in systems where resources have parent-child relationships and lock acquisition must respect these hierarchies to prevent deadlocks and ensure data integrity across distributed components.\n",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "uGgdvmV28TO9sD5Oe7WtE",
