			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bEB",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 177584234,
			"version": 603,
			"versionNonce": 1754693814,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "ZooKeeper is a distributed coordination service that helps manage configuration, naming, and synchronization for distributed applications. It provides a simple file system-like interface but packs sophisticated capabilities for managing configuration, service discovery, leader election, and distributed\nlocks. While newer alternatives like etcd, Consul, and cloud provider solutions exist, ZooKeeper's patterns in particular are fundamental to distributed systems design.\n\nBut be careful. You don't want to jump to ZooKeeper in your next system design interview unless you're designing a deep infrastructure system and need to discuss how to manage careful coordination across multiple servers or you need more advanced functionality than is offered out of the box\nby modern load balancers and built in service discovery tools.",
			"rawText": "ZooKeeper is a distributed coordination service that helps manage configuration, naming, and synchronization for distributed applications. It provides a simple file system-like interface but packs sophisticated capabilities for managing configuration, service discovery, leader election, and distributed locks. While newer alternatives like etcd, Consul, and cloud provider solutions exist, ZooKeeper's patterns in particular are fundamental to distributed systems design.\n\nBut be careful. You don't want to jump to ZooKeeper in your next system design interview unless you're designing a deep infrastructure system and need to discuss how to manage careful coordination across multiple servers or you need more advanced functionality than is offered out of the box by modern load balancers and built in service discovery tools.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "ZooKeeper is a distributed coordination service that helps manage configuration, naming, and synchronization for distributed applications. It provides a simple file system-like interface but packs sophisticated capabilities for managing configuration, service discovery, leader election, and distributed locks. While newer alternatives like etcd, Consul, and cloud provider solutions exist, ZooKeeper's patterns in particular are fundamental to distributed systems design.\n\nBut be careful. You don't want to jump to ZooKeeper in your next system design interview unless you're designing a deep infrastructure system and need to discuss how to manage careful coordination across multiple servers or you need more advanced functionality than is offered out of the box by modern load balancers and built in service discovery tools.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "DAua4QvC",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -488.6009739167016,
			"y": 21531.40566891158,
			"width": 112.3919677734375,
			"height": 35,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bEC",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 550968106,
			"version": 428,
			"versionNonce": 1320221174,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"link": null,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Summary",
			"rawText": "Summary",
			"fontSize": 28,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Summary",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false
		},
		{
			"id": "Z6AfV0eg",
			"type": "text",
			"x": 650.4011238709576,
			"y": 264.449184544402,
			"width": 769.4117431640625,
			"height": 60,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bED",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 375268842,
			"version": 512,
			"versionNonce": 363481910,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"link": null,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Initially, your chat app runs on a single server. Life is simple. When Alice sends a message to Bob,\nboth users are connected to the same server. The server knows exactly where to deliver the\nmessage - it's all in-memory, low latency, no coordination needed.",
			"rawText": "Initially, your chat app runs on a single server. Life is simple. When Alice sends a message to Bob, both users are connected to the same server. The server knows exactly where to deliver the message - it's all in-memory, low latency, no coordination needed.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Initially, your chat app runs on a single server. Life is simple. When Alice sends a message to Bob, both users are connected to the same server. The server knows exactly where to deliver the message - it's all in-memory, low latency, no coordination needed.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false
		},
		{
			"id": "LBf7zrjM",
			"type": "text",
			"x": 673.0784309198203,
			"y": 858.9719824729775,
			"width": 795.9352416992188,
			"height": 80,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bEE",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1144379562,
			"version": 438,
			"versionNonce": 1810330742,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"link": null,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "But your app gets popular. A single server can't handle the load anymore, so you add a second server.\nNow you have a problem: when Alice (connected to Server 1) sends a message to Bob (connected to\nServer 2), Server 1 needs to know where Bob is located to deliver the message. How does Server 1\ndiscover which server Bob is connected to?",
			"rawText": "But your app gets popular. A single server can't handle the load anymore, so you add a second server. Now you have a problem: when Alice (connected to Server 1) sends a message to Bob (connected to Server 2), Server 1 needs to know where Bob is located to deliver the message. How does Server 1 discover which server Bob is connected to?",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "But your app gets popular. A single server can't handle the load anymore, so you add a second server. Now you have a problem: when Alice (connected to Server 1) sends a message to Bob (connected to Server 2), Server 1 needs to know where Bob is located to deliver the message. How does Server 1 discover which server Bob is connected to?",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false
		},
		{
			"id": "W9eocVFH",
			"type": "text",
			"x": 994.9972744853808,
			"y": 1444.4235785292672,
			"width": 1034.6505603854569,
			"height": 120,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bEF",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 705246058,
			"version": 620,
			"versionNonce": 1798141366,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "You might think, \"I'll just use a database!\" Store a table that maps users to their servers. When a user connects, update the\ndatabase. When a message needs routing, check the database. Simple, right?\n\nBut this introduces new problems. The database becomes a single point of failure. If it goes down, your entire chat system breaks.\nIt also adds latency - every message now requires a database lookup before routing. As your system scales to millions of users,\nthis database gets hammered with queries, creating a bottleneck.",
			"rawText": "You might think, \"I'll just use a database!\" Store a table that maps users to their servers. When a user connects, update the database. When a message needs routing, check the database. Simple, right?\n\nBut this introduces new problems. The database becomes a single point of failure. If it goes down, your entire chat system breaks. It also adds latency - every message now requires a database lookup before routing. As your system scales to millions of users, this database gets hammered with queries, creating a bottleneck.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "You might think, \"I'll just use a database!\" Store a table that maps users to their servers. When a user connects, update the database. When a message needs routing, check the database. Simple, right?\n\nBut this introduces new problems. The database becomes a single point of failure. If it goes down, your entire chat system breaks. It also adds latency - every message now requires a database lookup before routing. As your system scales to millions of users, this database gets hammered with queries, creating a bottleneck.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "Usvjzelh",
			"type": "text",
			"x": 1000.8848046470015,
			"y": 2026.865333598667,
			"width": 1014.9476781277222,
			"height": 120,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bEG",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 438515242,
			"version": 693,
			"versionNonce": 748769014,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942921,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "You might try to optimize with caching, but now you have cache consistency issues. What if a user disconnects from Server 2\nand reconnects to Server 3, but Server 1's cache still thinks they're on Server 2? Messages get lost.\n\nSo you think, \"I'll have servers directly tell each other about changes!\" When a user connects to Server 3, it broadcasts to all\nother servers. But as your system grows to dozens or hundreds of servers, these broadcasts create a lot of network traffic.\nEvery server has to maintain connections with every other server - that's n² connections, which doesn't scale well.",
			"rawText": "You might try to optimize with caching, but now you have cache consistency issues. What if a user disconnects from Server 2 and reconnects to Server 3, but Server 1's cache still thinks they're on Server 2? Messages get lost.\n\nSo you think, \"I'll have servers directly tell each other about changes!\" When a user connects to Server 3, it broadcasts to all other servers. But as your system grows to dozens or hundreds of servers, these broadcasts create a lot of network traffic. Every server has to maintain connections with every other server - that's n² connections, which doesn't scale well.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "You might try to optimize with caching, but now you have cache consistency issues. What if a user disconnects from Server 2 and reconnects to Server 3, but Server 1's cache still thinks they're on Server 2? Messages get lost.\n\nSo you think, \"I'll have servers directly tell each other about changes!\" When a user connects to Server 3, it broadcasts to all other servers. But as your system grows to dozens or hundreds of servers, these broadcasts create a lot of network traffic. Every server has to maintain connections with every other server - that's n² connections, which doesn't scale well.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "BEhqkOjP",
			"type": "text",
			"x": 1360.3697127835028,
			"y": 2511.109231298627,
			"width": 1251.5707690252812,
			"height": 360,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "hachure",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "bEH",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1701765354,
			"version": 784,
			"versionNonce": 1556611126,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1775169942922,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Then you face the server failure problem. What if Server 2 crashes? It can't tell anyone it's down. Users connected to it are disconnected, but other servers\ndon't know that. They keep trying to send messages to a dead server.\n\nSo you implement heartbeats - servers periodically check if other servers are alive. But this creates a new issue: the famous \"network partition\" problem. What\nif Server 2 is alive but there's a network issue between it and Server 1? Server 1 thinks Server 2 is dead, but it's not. Some servers might see Server 2 as alive\nwhile others see it as dead. You now have an inconsistent view of your system state.\n\nWhile a chat application could alternatively use pub/sub patterns to avoid some coordination issues, we're focusing on scenarios where direct coordination is\nnecessary to illustrate ZooKeeper's capabilities. Many real-world systems need the kind of coordination problems we're discussing here.\n\nEach of these problems - service discovery, configuration sharing, failure detection, leader election, and distributed consensus - are fundamental challenges in\ndistributed systems. Solving them correctly is incredibly difficult, requiring algorithms that can handle partial failures, network delays, and still maintain\nconsistency. This is precisely the set of problems ZooKeeper was built to solve.\n\nZooKeeper solves all the problems we've discussed above. It provides a consistent, reliable source of truth that all servers can trust. When servers come\nonline, they register in ZooKeeper. When users connect to servers, that mapping gets stored in ZooKeeper. ZooKeeper then notifies interested servers about\nchanges and automatically handles failures through its ephemeral nodes. It gives you reliable service discovery, configuration management, and leader election\nwithout building these complex distributed algorithms yourself.",
			"rawText": "Then you face the server failure problem. What if Server 2 crashes? It can't tell anyone it's down. Users connected to it are disconnected, but other servers don't know that. They keep trying to send messages to a dead server.\n\nSo you implement heartbeats - servers periodically check if other servers are alive. But this creates a new issue: the famous \"network partition\" problem. What if Server 2 is alive but there's a network issue between it and Server 1? Server 1 thinks Server 2 is dead, but it's not. Some servers might see Server 2 as alive while others see it as dead. You now have an inconsistent view of your system state.\n\nWhile a chat application could alternatively use pub/sub patterns to avoid some coordination issues, we're focusing on scenarios where direct coordination is necessary to illustrate ZooKeeper's capabilities. Many real-world systems need the kind of coordination problems we're discussing here.\n\nEach of these problems - service discovery, configuration sharing, failure detection, leader election, and distributed consensus - are fundamental challenges in distributed systems. Solving them correctly is incredibly difficult, requiring algorithms that can handle partial failures, network delays, and still maintain consistency. This is precisely the set of problems ZooKeeper was built to solve.\n\nZooKeeper solves all the problems we've discussed above. It provides a consistent, reliable source of truth that all servers can trust. When servers come online, they register in ZooKeeper. When users connect to servers, that mapping gets stored in ZooKeeper. ZooKeeper then notifies interested servers about changes and automatically handles failures through its ephemeral nodes. It gives you reliable service discovery, configuration management, and leader election without building these complex distributed algorithms yourself.",
			"fontSize": 16,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Then you face the server failure problem. What if Server 2 crashes? It can't tell anyone it's down. Users connected to it are disconnected, but other servers don't know that. They keep trying to send messages to a dead server.\n\nSo you implement heartbeats - servers periodically check if other servers are alive. But this creates a new issue: the famous \"network partition\" problem. What if Server 2 is alive but there's a network issue between it and Server 1? Server 1 thinks Server 2 is dead, but it's not. Some servers might see Server 2 as alive while others see it as dead. You now have an inconsistent view of your system state.\n\nWhile a chat application could alternatively use pub/sub patterns to avoid some coordination issues, we're focusing on scenarios where direct coordination is necessary to illustrate ZooKeeper's capabilities. Many real-world systems need the kind of coordination problems we're discussing here.\n\nEach of these problems - service discovery, configuration sharing, failure detection, leader election, and distributed consensus - are fundamental challenges in distributed systems. Solving them correctly is incredibly difficult, requiring algorithms that can handle partial failures, network delays, and still maintain consistency. This is precisely the set of problems ZooKeeper was built to solve.\n\nZooKeeper solves all the problems we've discussed above. It provides a consistent, reliable source of truth that all servers can trust. When servers come online, they register in ZooKeeper. When users connect to servers, that mapping gets stored in ZooKeeper. ZooKeeper then notifies interested servers about changes and automatically handles failures through its ephemeral nodes. It gives you reliable service discovery, configuration management, and leader election without building these complex distributed algorithms yourself.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		}
	],
